<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  10.10 The Binomial Series and Applications of Taylor Series
-->
<section xml:id="chapter-binomial-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>The Binomial Series and Applications of Taylor Series</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Write down the binomial series for <m>(1+x)^m</m> and compute the
binomial coefficients <m>\binom{m}{k}</m> for any real power <m>m</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Explain why the series terminates when <m>m</m> is a non-negative
integer and is infinite otherwise.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Expand functions such as <m>(x+1)^5</m>, <m>(x+1)^{-2}</m>, and
<m>(x+1)^{1/2}</m> as binomial series.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
We have already seen one of the applications of the Taylor series,
namely approximating a function by a polynomial of degree <m>n</m>. In
this section we study two more applications of the Taylor series: the
binomial series and estimating/evaluating non-elementary integrals.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-binomial-series">
<title>Binomial Series</title>

<p>
Before we try powers such as <m>(1+x)^{-2}</m> or
<m>\sqrt{1+x}</m>, let us look at what happens when the exponent is an
ordinary positive integer, where nothing beyond multiplication is
needed. Multiplying out by hand,

<md>
<mrow>(1+x)^1 \amp = 1 + x,</mrow>
<mrow>(1+x)^2 \amp = 1 + 2x + x^2,</mrow>
<mrow>(1+x)^3 \amp = 1 + 3x + 3x^2 + x^3,</mrow>
<mrow>(1+x)^4 \amp = 1 + 4x + 6x^2 + 4x^3 + x^4.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Two features stand out. First, each expansion <em>stops</em>: the power
<m>(1+x)^n</m> gives a polynomial with exactly <m>n+1</m> terms.
Second, the coefficients are far from random. Stripping them out and
stacking one row per exponent gives the array in
<xref ref="fig-pascals-triangle"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pascals-triangle">
<caption>
Pascal's triangle. Row <m>n</m> holds the coefficients of
<m>(1+x)^n</m>. Every row begins and ends with <m>1</m>, and each
remaining entry is the sum of the two entries above it; the arrows
show the <m>3 + 3 = 6</m> of the last row.
</caption>
<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pascals-triangle">
<diagram dimensions="(200,180)" margins="8">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.9,-3.15,1.9,0.4)">
<group at="pt-row-0">
  <label anchor="(0.00,-0.00)" alignment="c"><m>1</m></label>
</group>
<group at="pt-row-1">
  <label anchor="(-0.40,-0.70)" alignment="c"><m>1</m></label>
  <label anchor="(0.40,-0.70)" alignment="c"><m>1</m></label>
</group>
<group at="pt-row-2">
  <label anchor="(-0.80,-1.40)" alignment="c"><m>1</m></label>
  <label anchor="(0.00,-1.40)" alignment="c"><m>2</m></label>
  <label anchor="(0.80,-1.40)" alignment="c"><m>1</m></label>
</group>
<group at="pt-row-3">
  <label anchor="(-1.20,-2.10)" alignment="c"><m>1</m></label>
  <label anchor="(-0.40,-2.10)" alignment="c"><m>3</m></label>
  <label anchor="(0.40,-2.10)" alignment="c"><m>3</m></label>
  <label anchor="(1.20,-2.10)" alignment="c"><m>1</m></label>
</group>
<group at="pt-row-4">
  <label anchor="(-1.60,-2.80)" alignment="c"><m>1</m></label>
  <label anchor="(-0.80,-2.80)" alignment="c"><m>4</m></label>
  <label anchor="(0.00,-2.80)" alignment="c"><m>6</m></label>
  <label anchor="(0.80,-2.80)" alignment="c"><m>4</m></label>
  <label anchor="(1.60,-2.80)" alignment="c"><m>1</m></label>
</group>
<group at="pt-addition">
  <line p1="(-0.40,-2.30)" p2="(-0.09,-2.58)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(0.40,-2.30)" p2="(0.09,-2.58)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
</group>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
  <annotation ref="figure" text="Pascal's triangle, five rows, arranged so that each row is centered below the one before it. Row n holds the coefficients of one plus x to the n. Every row begins and ends with one, and each remaining entry is the sum of the two entries just above it.">
    <annotation ref="pt-row-0" text="Row 0: one. These are the coefficients of one plus x to the zero, which is one."/>
    <annotation ref="pt-row-1" text="Row 1: one, one. These are the coefficients of one plus x."/>
    <annotation ref="pt-row-2" text="Row 2: one, two, one. These are the coefficients of one plus x squared."/>
    <annotation ref="pt-row-3" text="Row 3: one, three, three, one. These are the coefficients of one plus x cubed."/>
    <annotation ref="pt-row-4" text="Row 4: one, four, six, four, one. These are the coefficients of one plus x to the fourth."/>
    <annotation ref="pt-addition" text="Two arrows showing the addition rule: the three and the three in row three point down to the six directly below them in row four, because three plus three is six."/>
  </annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
This array is called <term>Pascal's triangle</term>. Every row starts
and ends with <m>1</m>, and each remaining entry is the sum of the two
entries just above it <mdash/> the <m>6</m> in the bottom row, for
instance, is <m>3 + 3</m>. That rule alone produces the next expansion
with no multiplying at all: the following row is
<m>1,\, 5,\, 10,\, 10,\, 5,\, 1</m>, and therefore
<m>(1+x)^5 = 1 + 5x + 10x^2 + 10x^3 + 5x^4 + x^5</m>.
</p>

<p>
The triangle tells us <em>what</em> the coefficients are, but not yet
<em>why</em>. For that, look at where a term of the expansion actually
comes from. Written out in full, the fourth power is

<md xml:id="eq-fourth-power" number="yes">
(1+x)^4 = (1+x)(1+x)(1+x)(1+x),
</md>

and multiplying this out means walking through the four factors,
picking either the <m>1</m> or the <m>x</m> out of each one, and
multiplying the four picks together. Every term of the expansion arises
that way, from one such set of picks. So to land on <m>x^3</m> we must
take the <m>x</m> from exactly three of the four factors, and the
<m>1</m> from the single factor left over. The question <q>what is the
coefficient of <m>x^3</m>?</q> has turned into a counting question:
<em>in how many ways can we choose <m>3</m> of the <m>4</m> factors to
hand us an <m>x</m>?</em>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-binomial-choose-x3">
<caption>
The four ways to produce <m>x^3</m> from
<m>(1+x)(1+x)(1+x)(1+x)</m>. Each row picks one term out of every
factor: an <m>x</m> from three of them (colored arrows) and the
<m>1</m> from the fourth (gray arrow). Four rows, so the coefficient
of <m>x^3</m> is <m>4</m>.
</caption>
<image width="100%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-binomial-choose-x3">
<diagram dimensions="(640,420)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(0.4,-6.4,12.4,0.9)">
<group at="row-1">
  <label anchor="(1.60,-0.00)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(3.60,-0.00)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(5.60,-0.00)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(7.60,-0.00)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <line p1="(1.89,-0.90)" p2="(1.89,-0.30)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(3.89,-0.90)" p2="(3.89,-0.30)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(5.89,-0.90)" p2="(5.89,-0.30)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(7.30,-0.90)" p2="(7.30,-0.30)" stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <label anchor="(9.10,-0.00)" alignment="e"><m>x\cdot x\cdot x\cdot 1 = x^3</m></label>
</group>
<group at="row-2">
  <label anchor="(1.60,-1.75)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(3.60,-1.75)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(5.60,-1.75)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(7.60,-1.75)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <line p1="(1.89,-2.65)" p2="(1.89,-2.05)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(3.89,-2.65)" p2="(3.89,-2.05)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(7.89,-2.65)" p2="(7.89,-2.05)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(5.30,-2.65)" p2="(5.30,-2.05)" stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <label anchor="(9.10,-1.75)" alignment="e"><m>x\cdot x\cdot 1\cdot x = x^3</m></label>
</group>
<group at="row-3">
  <label anchor="(1.60,-3.50)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(3.60,-3.50)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(5.60,-3.50)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(7.60,-3.50)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <line p1="(1.89,-4.40)" p2="(1.89,-3.80)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(5.89,-4.40)" p2="(5.89,-3.80)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(7.89,-4.40)" p2="(7.89,-3.80)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(3.30,-4.40)" p2="(3.30,-3.80)" stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <label anchor="(9.10,-3.50)" alignment="e"><m>x\cdot 1\cdot x\cdot x = x^3</m></label>
</group>
<group at="row-4">
  <label anchor="(1.60,-5.25)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(3.60,-5.25)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(5.60,-5.25)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <label anchor="(7.60,-5.25)" alignment="c"><m>(1+x)</m></label>
  <line p1="(3.89,-6.15)" p2="(3.89,-5.55)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(5.89,-6.15)" p2="(5.89,-5.55)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(7.89,-6.15)" p2="(7.89,-5.55)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <line p1="(1.30,-6.15)" p2="(1.30,-5.55)" stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
  <label anchor="(9.10,-5.25)" alignment="e"><m>1\cdot x\cdot x\cdot x = x^3</m></label>
</group>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
  <annotation ref="figure" text="Four rows. Each row shows the product one plus x written four times, with arrows pointing up at the one term picked out of each factor: an x from three of the factors, drawn as orange arrows, and the one from the remaining factor, drawn as a gray arrow. Every row multiplies out to x cubed, and the four rows are the only ways to do it, so the coefficient of x cubed is four.">
    <annotation ref="row-1" text="The first way: the x is taken from the first, second and third factors, and the one from the fourth factor. Multiplying the four picks gives x times x times x times one, which is x cubed."/>
    <annotation ref="row-2" text="The second way: the x is taken from the first, second and fourth factors, and the one from the third factor. Multiplying the four picks gives x times x times one times x, which is x cubed."/>
    <annotation ref="row-3" text="The third way: the x is taken from the first, third and fourth factors, and the one from the second factor. Multiplying the four picks gives x times one times x times x, which is x cubed."/>
    <annotation ref="row-4" text="The fourth way: the x is taken from the second, third and fourth factors, and the one from the first factor. Multiplying the four picks gives one times x times x times x, which is x cubed."/>
  </annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
There are four such choices and no others, so multiplying out
<xref ref="eq-fourth-power"/> produces <m>x^3</m> four times and its
coefficient is <m>4</m> <mdash/> exactly the entry
sitting in the row <m>1,\, 4,\, 6,\, 4,\, 1</m>. Nothing here was
special to <m>x^3</m> or to the fourth power. In <m>(1+x)^n</m> the
coefficient of <m>x^k</m> counts the ways of choosing <m>k</m> of the
<m>n</m> factors to supply an <m>x</m>, the remaining <m>n-k</m>
factors supplying a <m>1</m>. That count is written
<m>\binom{n}{k}</m> and read <q>n choose k</q>, and counting the
choices gives the formula

<md xml:id="eq-binom-factorial" number="yes">
\binom{n}{k} = \frac{n!}{k!\,(n-k)!}.
</md>

The addition rule we noticed in the triangle is a consequence of the
same count.<fn>
  Sort the four rows of <xref ref="fig-binomial-choose-x3"/> by what
  the <em>last</em> factor contributes. In three of them it hands over
  its <m>x</m>, so the two remaining <m>x</m>'s come from the first
  three factors; there are <m>\binom{3}{2} = 3</m> ways to pick which
  two, namely <m>\{1,2\}</m>, <m>\{1,3\}</m> and <m>\{2,3\}</m>, and
  these are precisely the second, third and fourth rows of the figure.
  In the row left over the last factor hands over its <m>1</m>, so all
  three <m>x</m>'s come from the first three factors, and there is
  only <m>\binom{3}{3} = 1</m> way to do that. Hence
  <m>\binom{4}{3} = \binom{3}{2} + \binom{3}{3} = 3 + 1 = 4</m>, which
  is why the <m>4</m> in the row <m>1,\, 4,\, 6,\, 4,\, 1</m> sits
  directly below the <m>3</m> and the <m>1</m> above it. Nothing
  depended on the particular numbers: a choice of <m>k</m> factors out
  of <m>n</m> either uses the last factor or it does not, so
  <m>\binom{n}{k} = \binom{n-1}{k-1} + \binom{n-1}{k}</m> in general.
</fn>
</p>

<p>
Putting the count together with the expansion gives the
<term>Binomial Theorem</term>:

<md xml:id="eq-binomial-theorem" number="yes">
(1+x)^n = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k} x^k
= 1 + nx + \frac{n(n-1)}{2!}x^2 + \cdots + nx^{n-1} + x^n.
</md>
</p>

<p>
But what if the exponent is <em>not</em> a positive integer? Could we
expand something like <m>(1+x)^{-2}</m>, or
<m>\sqrt{1+x} = (1+x)^{1/2}</m>, in the same spirit? At first the idea
seems doomed: the factorial formula <xref ref="eq-binom-factorial"/>
makes no sense when <m>n = \frac12</m>. However, the equivalent <q>falling product</q> form

<md xml:id="eq-binom-falling" number="yes">
\binom{m}{k} = \frac{m(m-1)(m-2)\cdots(m-k+1)}{k!}
</md>

asks only that we start at <m>m</m> and multiply <m>k</m> numbers,
each one less than the previous <mdash/> and that recipe works for
<em>any</em> real number <m>m</m>. It also explains why the Binomial Theorem
<xref ref="eq-binomial-theorem"/> terminates: when <m>m</m> is a non-negative integer, the
factor <m>(m-m) = 0</m> eventually appears in the product, killing
every coefficient from <m>k = m+1</m> on. When <m>m</m> is negative or
fractional, no factor is ever zero, so the expansion never stops:
instead of a polynomial we obtain an infinite power series. This
bold step beyond the Binomial Theorem goes back to Newton, and Taylor
series give us exactly the right framework to justify it.
</p>

<p>
Indeed, the Taylor series generated by <m>f(x) = (1+x)^m</m> at
<m>x = 0</m> is

<md xml:id="eq-binomial-taylor" number="yes">
1 + mx + \frac{m(m-1)}{2!}x^2 + \cdots
+ \frac{m(m-1)(m-2)\cdots(m-k+1)}{k!}x^k + \cdots,
</md>

with precisely the coefficients that <xref ref="eq-binom-falling"/>
predicts. The series <xref ref="eq-binomial-taylor"/> is known as the
<term>binomial series</term>.
</p>


<assemblage xml:id="summary-binomial-series">
<title>Binomial Series</title>
<p>
For <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m>,
<md xml:id="eq-binomial-series" number="yes">
(1+x)^m = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{m}{k} x^k
</md>
where
<md>
\binom{m}{1} = m, \qquad
\binom{m}{2} = \frac{m(m-1)}{2},
</md>
and
<md>
<mrow>\binom{m}{k} \amp = \frac{m!}{k!(m-k)!}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{m(m-1)(m-2)\cdots(m-k+2)(m-k+1)}{k!}
\quad\text{for } k \ge 3.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
<term>Remark:</term> The binomial series <xref ref="eq-binomial-series"/>
converges for
<m>|x| \lt 1</m>. We will not prove this in these notes.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-binomial-positive">
<title>The power is a positive integer</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use binomial series to write the function <m>f(x) = (x+1)^5</m> as a
series.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We apply the binomial series <xref ref="eq-binomial-series"/> with
<m>m = 5</m>:

<md>
<mrow>(1+x)^5 \amp = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{5}{k} x^k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 + \binom{5}{1}x + \binom{5}{2}x^2
+ \binom{5}{3}x^3 + \binom{5}{4}x^4</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \binom{5}{5}x^5 + \binom{5}{6}x^6
+ \binom{5}{7}x^7 + \cdots</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that for <m>k = 6</m>,
<md>
\binom{5}{6} = \frac{5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1 \cdot 0}{6!} = 0,
</md>
in fact for any <m>k \gt 5</m>, it is true that
<m>\binom{5}{k} = 0</m> and therefore

<md>
<mrow>(1+x)^5 \amp = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{5}{k} x^k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 + \binom{5}{1}x + \binom{5}{2}x^2 + \binom{5}{3}x^3
+ \binom{5}{4}x^4 + \binom{5}{5}x^5</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 + 5x + 10x^2 + 10x^3 + 5x^4 + x^5</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-binomial-negative">
<title>The power is a negative integer</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use binomial series to write the function <m>f(x) = (x+1)^{-2}</m> as a
series.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We apply the binomial series <xref ref="eq-binomial-series"/> with
<m>m = -2</m>:

<md>
(1+x)^{-2} = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{-2}{k} x^k
</md>
</p>

<p>
The first coefficients are

<md>
\binom{-2}{1} = -2, \qquad
\binom{-2}{2} = \frac{(-2)(-2-1)}{2} = 3,
</md>
</p>

<p>
and for general <m>k</m>,

<md>
<mrow>\binom{-2}{k} \amp
= \frac{(-2)(-2-1)(-2-2)(-2-3)\cdots(-2-k+1)}{k!}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (-1)^k \frac{(k+1)!}{k!} = (-1)^k (k+1).</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Hence

<md>
<mrow>(1+x)^{-2} \amp = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{-2}{k} x^k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 - 2x + 3x^2 + \sum_{k=3}^{\infty} (-1)^k (k+1) x^k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 - 2x + 3x^2 - 4x^3 + 5x^4 - 6x^5 + \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + (-1)^k (k+1) x^k + \cdots</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that this series converges if <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-binomial-fractional">
<title>The power is not an integer</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use binomial series to write the function
<m>f(x) = (x+1)^{\frac12}</m> as a series. Compute the five non-zero
terms.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We apply the binomial series <xref ref="eq-binomial-series"/> with
<m>m = \frac12</m>:

<md>
(1+x)^{\frac12} = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{\frac12}{k} x^k
</md>
</p>

<p>
The coefficients are

<md>
<mrow>\binom{\frac12}{1} \amp = \frac12,</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{\frac12}{2} \amp
= \frac{\frac12\left(\frac12 - 1\right)}{2}
= \frac{\frac12\left(-\frac12\right)}{2},</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{\frac12}{3} \amp
= \frac{\frac12\left(\frac12 - 1\right)\left(\frac12 - 2\right)}{3!}
= \frac{\frac12\left(-\frac12\right)\left(-\frac32\right)}{3!},</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{\frac12}{4} \amp
= \frac{\frac12\left(\frac12 - 1\right)\left(\frac12 - 2\right)
\left(\frac12 - 3\right)}{4!}
= \frac{\frac12\left(-\frac12\right)\left(-\frac32\right)
\left(-\frac52\right)}{4!}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Hence the five non-zero terms are

<md>
(1+x)^{\frac12}
= 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{\frac12}{k} x^k
= 1 + \frac12 x - \frac{x^2}{8} + \frac{x^3}{16}
- \frac{5x^4}{128} + \cdots
</md>
</p>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-binomial-sqrt"/> shows the function
<m>y = \sqrt{1+x}</m> together with the partial sums of this series on
the interval of convergence <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-binomial-sqrt">
<caption>
The function <m>y = \sqrt{1+x}</m> and the partial sums of its binomial
series with two, three, and five terms. On the interval
<m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m> the partial sums approach the function.
</caption>
<image width="78%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-binomial-sqrt">
<diagram dimensions="(400,280)" margins="12">
<definition>f(t) = (t, (1 + t)^(1/2))</definition>
<definition>q1(t) = (t, 1 + t/2)</definition>
<definition>q2(t) = (t, 1 + t/2 - t^2/8)</definition>
<definition>q4(t) = (t, 1 + t/2 - t^2/8 + t^3/16 - 5*t^4/128)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.3,-0.35,1.3,1.9)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-q1" function="q1" domain="(-1.0,1.0)"
                  stroke="#E69F00" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-q2" function="q2" domain="(-1.0,1.0)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-q4" function="q4" domain="(-1.0,1.0)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-f" function="f" domain="(-0.998,1.0)"
                  stroke="black" thickness="3" dash="7 4"/>
<label at="bs-f-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.78)" alignment="e">
<m>y=\sqrt{1+x}</m>
</label>
<label at="bs-q1-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.55)" alignment="e" color="#E69F00">
<m>1+\frac{x}{2}</m>
</label>
<label at="bs-q2-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.32)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>1+\frac{x}{2}-\frac{x^2}{8}</m>
</label>
<label at="bs-q4-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.09)" alignment="e" color="#009E73">
<m>\text{five terms}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The curve y equals the square root of one plus x, drawn as a dashed black line on the interval from negative one to one, together with three partial sums of its binomial series: the linear approximation in orange, the quadratic in blue, and the five term partial sum in green. All curves pass through zero comma one, and the five term sum is nearly indistinguishable from the function across the interval.">
  <annotation ref="bs-f" text="The function y equals the square root of one plus x, dashed black."/>
  <annotation ref="bs-q1" text="The two term partial sum, one plus x over two."/>
  <annotation ref="bs-q2" text="The three term partial sum, which adds the quadratic correction."/>
  <annotation ref="bs-q4" text="The five term partial sum, which is very close to the function on the whole interval."/>
</annotation>
