</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-binomial-applications">
<title>Applications</title>

<p>
Many integrals that arise in practice cannot be expressed in terms of
elementary functions. Replacing the integrand by its Taylor series and
integrating term by term produces a series for the integral, and the
partial sums of that series give numerical estimates whose accuracy we
can control. Taylor series also give an efficient way to evaluate
limits of indeterminate form, often faster than repeated applications
of l'H&#xf4;pital's rule.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-binomial-integral-estimate">
<title>Estimating a non-elementary integral</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use the binomial series to estimate

<md>
\int_0^{1/3} \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^3}} \, dx,
</md>

and compute an upper bound for the error of your estimate.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The antiderivative of <m>\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^3}}</m> is not an
elementary function, so we replace the integrand by its binomial
series. We apply the binomial series <xref ref="eq-binomial-series"/>
with <m>m = -\frac12</m>, substituting <m>-x^3</m> for <m>x</m>:

<md>
(1-x^3)^{-\frac12}
= 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{-\frac12}{k} (-x^3)^k.
</md>
</p>

<p>
The first coefficients are

<md>
<mrow>\binom{-\frac12}{1}(-1)^1 \amp = \frac12,</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{-\frac12}{2}(-1)^2 \amp
= \frac{\left(-\frac12\right)\left(-\frac32\right)}{2!}
= \frac{3}{8},</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{-\frac12}{3}(-1)^3 \amp
= -\frac{\left(-\frac12\right)\left(-\frac32\right)
\left(-\frac52\right)}{3!}
= \frac{5}{16},</mrow>
</md>

so that for <m>|x| \lt 1</m>,

<md xml:id="eq-integrand-series" number="yes">
\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^3}}
= 1 + \frac{x^3}{2} + \frac{3x^6}{8} + \frac{5x^9}{16} + \cdots.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Integrating <xref ref="eq-integrand-series"/> term by term,

<md>
<mrow>\int_0^{1/3} \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^3}} \, dx
\amp = \left[ x + \frac{x^4}{8} + \frac{3x^7}{56}
+ \frac{x^{10}}{32} + \cdots \right]_0^{1/3}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac13 + \frac{1}{8 \cdot 3^4}
+ \frac{3}{56 \cdot 3^7} + \frac{1}{32 \cdot 3^{10}} + \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac13 + \frac{1}{648} + \frac{1}{40824}
+ \frac{1}{1889568} + \cdots.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Using the first two terms,

<md>
\int_0^{1/3} \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^3}} \, dx
\approx \frac13 + \frac{1}{648} = \frac{217}{648} \approx 0.334877.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>Upper bound for the error.</term> All the terms of the series
are positive, so the error of the estimate is exactly the sum of the
omitted terms, beginning with <m>a_2 = \frac{1}{40824}</m>. Each term
of the series contains a factor <m>x^{3}</m> more than the one before
it, and on our interval <m>x^3 \le \left(\frac13\right)^3 =
\frac{1}{27}</m>; since the remaining factors in the ratio of
consecutive terms are all less than <m>1</m>, each omitted term is
less than <m>\frac{1}{27}</m> times the previous one. Comparing with a
geometric series with ratio <m>\frac{1}{27}</m>,

<md>
<mrow>\text{Error} \amp \lt a_2\left(1 + \frac{1}{27}
+ \frac{1}{27^2} + \cdots\right)
= \frac{1}{40824} \cdot \frac{1}{1 - \frac{1}{27}}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{1}{40824} \cdot \frac{27}{26}
\lt 2.6 \times 10^{-5}.</mrow>
</md>

Therefore the two-term estimate <m>0.334877</m> is correct to within
<m>2.6 \times 10^{-5}</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-binomial-limit">
<title>Evaluating a limit</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use series to evaluate

<md>
\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\sqrt{1 + \sin x} - 1 - \frac{x}{2}}{x^2}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
From <xref ref="example-binomial-fractional"/> we have the binomial
series

<md xml:id="eq-sqrt-series" number="yes">
\sqrt{1+u} = 1 + \frac{u}{2} - \frac{u^2}{8} + \frac{u^3}{16}
- \cdots, \qquad |u| \lt 1.
</md>

Near <m>x = 0</m> the quantity <m>\sin x</m> is small, so we may
substitute <m>u = \sin x</m> in <xref ref="eq-sqrt-series"/>:

<md>
\sqrt{1 + \sin x}
= 1 + \frac{\sin x}{2} - \frac{\sin^2 x}{8} + \frac{\sin^3 x}{16}
- \cdots.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Now replace <m>\sin x</m> by its Maclaurin series
<m>\sin x = x - \frac{x^3}{3!} + \cdots</m>, so that
<m>\sin^2 x = x^2 - \frac{x^4}{3} + \cdots</m> and every later term
involves powers <m>x^3</m> and higher. Collecting powers of
<m>x</m>,

<md>
<mrow>\sqrt{1 + \sin x}
\amp = 1 + \frac12\left(x - \frac{x^3}{6} + \cdots\right)
- \frac18\left(x^2 - \cdots\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \frac{1}{16}\left(x^3 - \cdots\right) - \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 + \frac{x}{2} - \frac{x^2}{8}
- \frac{x^3}{48} + \cdots.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Hence the numerator is

<md>
\sqrt{1 + \sin x} - 1 - \frac{x}{2}
= -\frac{x^2}{8} - \frac{x^3}{48} + \cdots,
</md>

and dividing by <m>x^2</m> gives

<md>
<mrow>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\sqrt{1 + \sin x} - 1 - \frac{x}{2}}{x^2}
\amp = \lim_{x \to 0}\left(-\frac18 - \frac{x}{48} + \cdots\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -\frac18.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Notice that the constant terms and the <m>\frac{x}{2}</m> terms cancel
exactly; the limit is decided by the coefficient of <m>x^2</m> in the
series for <m>\sqrt{1+\sin x}</m>. Evaluating the same limit by
l'H&#xf4;pital's rule would require differentiating the numerator
twice, a much longer computation.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
